<Project InitialTargets="_ArchspecSelectAndCheck">
  <!-- archspec tools-package selection (decision-check record 2026-11-06;
       target-time RID selection for GitHub issue #42, decision-check
       record 2026-11-17).

       The package is tools-only: restore does not copy its files to the
       consumer output, so $(ArchspecPath) addresses the package folder
       directly through $(MSBuildThisFileDirectory) (finding M4 — the
       Grpc.Tools mechanism, applied to the documented runtimes/<rid>/native
       native-files layout).

       Selection follows the .NET SDK's own runtime identifier — the machine
       running the build, not the target runtime — READ AT TARGET-EXECUTION
       TIME. Issue #42 proved $(NETCoreSdkRuntimeIdentifier) evaluates to
       the empty string at the nuget.g.props import position on every SDK,
       so an evaluation-time seed resolves nothing; the seed, the RID table
       and the unknown-RID error therefore ride ONE InitialTargets target,
       where the property carries its real value. An evaluation-time
       loud-fail is impossible: restore evaluation never runs targets, so a
       restore-time error element would break every consumer (the issue #37
       class). Read ArchspecPath at target-execution time — an
       evaluation-time capture (-getProperty) is empty by design.

       $(NETCoreSdkPortableRuntimeIdentifier) was considered and REJECTED:
       it is libc-blind — a build on Alpine collapses musl onto the plain
       linux-arm64 value, the glibc-only row the GAP below refuses — where
       the SDK RID reports linux-musl-arm64 and falls into the loud
       unknown-RID error instead of a silent exec failure.

        RID-table maintenance law: a row lands only for a RID the RID graph
        folds onto a shipped binary AND some SDK build is measured to report
        it. arch-x64 joins on that evidence: the runtime.json graph folds
        arch-x64 -> arch -> linux-x64 (the Arch Linux system SDK RID), so an
        Arch SDK build selects the static musl binary. alpine-x64 is the
        named candidate class: the graph folds it onto a shipped binary
        through linux-musl-x64, but no SDK build is measured to report it —
        no row until a measurement lands.

       The linux-x64 binary is a static musl build: it runs on glibc and musl
       x64 hosts alike, so linux-musl-x64 and arch-x64 are folded onto it
       here the way the RID graph folds them at restore time (finding R4/M3).

       GAP: Alpine on arm64 (linux-musl-arm64) has no binary. The
       linux-arm64 binary is a glibc build that will not execute there, so
       that RID is deliberately NOT folded onto it — it falls into the
       unknown-RID error below instead of shipping a silent exec failure.
       A future aarch64-musl release asset lands a real row.

       An unknown RID fails loud: there is no fallback to linux, unix or any
       — a props file that picked some binary anyway would be a silent-pass
       hole (finding M3). The <Error> rides the target because MSBuild 18
       (.NET 10 SDK) refuses an <Error> directly under <Project> at
       evaluation time (issue #37), and because at evaluation time there is
       no RID at all (issue #42) — the target runs before any requested
       target does useful work, and restore stays green. -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ArchspecPackageRoot>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../</ArchspecPackageRoot>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <Target Name="_ArchspecSelectAndCheck">
    <PropertyGroup>
      <_ArchspecRid Condition="'$(_ArchspecRid)' == ''">$(NETCoreSdkRuntimeIdentifier)</_ArchspecRid>
      <ArchspecPath Condition="'$(_ArchspecRid)' == 'linux-x64' or '$(_ArchspecRid)' == 'linux-musl-x64' or '$(_ArchspecRid)' == 'arch-x64'">$(ArchspecPackageRoot)runtimes/linux-x64/native/archspec</ArchspecPath>
      <ArchspecPath Condition="'$(_ArchspecRid)' == 'linux-arm64'">$(ArchspecPackageRoot)runtimes/linux-arm64/native/archspec</ArchspecPath>
      <ArchspecPath Condition="'$(_ArchspecRid)' == 'osx-x64'">$(ArchspecPackageRoot)runtimes/osx-x64/native/archspec</ArchspecPath>
      <ArchspecPath Condition="'$(_ArchspecRid)' == 'osx-arm64'">$(ArchspecPackageRoot)runtimes/osx-arm64/native/archspec</ArchspecPath>
      <ArchspecPath Condition="'$(_ArchspecRid)' == 'win-x64'">$(ArchspecPackageRoot)runtimes/win-x64/native/archspec.exe</ArchspecPath>
    </PropertyGroup>
    <Error Condition="'$(ArchspecPath)' == ''" Text="archspec: the archspec tools package ships no binary for .NET SDK runtime identifier '$(_ArchspecRid)'. It ships linux-x64 (static musl — also serves linux-musl-x64 and arch-x64), linux-arm64 (glibc — no Alpine arm64 build yet), osx-x64, osx-arm64 and win-x64, and falls back to nothing by design. ArchspecPath resolves at target-execution time — an evaluation-time capture is empty by design, not a missing install. Set _ArchspecRid explicitly only when you have proven that binary runs on this machine." />
  </Target>
</Project>
